        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The International Space Station (ISS) Expedition Four mission was marked by a continuing increase in science activities, a total of three extravehicular activities (EVAs), and delivery and installation of the first piece of truss that serves as the backbone of the station. Expedition Four crewmembers, Expedition Commander Yury Onufrienko and Flight Engineers Daniel Bursch and Carl Walz, were brought to the space station aboard STS-108 on December 5, 2001. 
<P>
The number of scientific investigations aboard the ISS almost doubled during Expedition Four, and new equipment made the research outpost even more productive. The research complement grew from 18 to 26 NASA payloads, seven of them new to the ISS program. Of the 26, two were in fundamental biology, seven in human life sciences, six in microgravity science, six in space product development, and five were sponsored by the Office of Space Flight. 
<P>
These new experiments could lead to new insights in the fields of plant growth, embryo development, the long-term effects of space flight on humans, biotechnology, medicine, agriculture, electronics, and pharmaceutical manufacturing. Several experiments started on earlier Expeditions were returned to Earth, while several others continued to operate during Expedition Four. 
<P>
Expedition Four Shuttle flights brought several new experiments, as well as several experiments making repeat flights aboard the station to continue their research. The life sciences experiment that began during Expedition Four was <B>Astronauts in Extravehicular Activity Radiation Monitoring (EVARM)</B>, conducted to determine the levels of radiation received to the skin, eyes, and blood-forming organs during EVAs. 
<P>
Life sciences experiments continuing into Expedition Four from earlier missions were: 
<P>
<UL>
<LI><B>Hoffman Reflex</B> to study changes in neurovestibular function with the goal of determining if exercise could be made more effective during long-term space flight.</LI>
<LI><B>Crew Interactions</B> to identify and characterize interpersonal and cultural factors that may affect crew and ground support personnel performance during space station missions.</LI>
<LI><B>Renal Stone</B> to study a possible countermeasure for kidney stone formation.</LI>
<LI></B>Pulmonary Function in Flight</B> experiment to examine long-term lung function in microgravity.</LI>
<LI><B>Sub-regional Assessment of Bone Loss in the Axial Skeleton in Long-term Space Flight</B> to measure bone loss and recovery for ISS crewmembers.</LI>
<LI><B>Xenon 1</B> to study blood flow and the body's ability to adjust to Earth's gravity after returning from space flight.</LI>
</UL>
<P>
Three spacewalks were conducted during the Expedition Four mission. During the first spacewalk, Onufrienko and Walz relocated the cargo boom for the Russian Strela crane. They moved the boom from Pressurized Mating Adapter 1 to the exterior of the Pirs Docking Compartment. The crew also installed an amateur radio antenna onto the end of the Zvezda Service Module. The spacewalk was based out of Pirs and used Russian Orlan space suits. 
<P>
During Expedition Four's second spacewalk, Onufrienko and Bursch installed six deflector shields for the Zvezda Service Module's jet thrusters. They also installed an amateur radio antenna, attached four science experiments, and retrieved and replaced a device to measure material from the thrusters. Like the first EVA, this one was based out of Pirs, and the spacewalkers used Orlan suits. 
<P>
The third spacewalk was based out of the Quest Airlock using U.S. space suits. Walz and Bursch tested the airlock and prepared for the four spacewalks that were to be performed during STS-110. The STS-110 crew installed the S0 Integrated Truss Structure onto the station. This spacewalk was the first spacewalk to be based out of Quest without a Space Shuttle docked at the station. 
<P>
As the end of Expedition Four approached, a Russian taxi crew brought a new Soyuz crew return vehicle to the station, and preparations were made for the arrival of the Expedition Five crew on STS-111. Expedition Four ended as STS-111 undocked from the station and returned to Earth on June 19, 2002.
        </div>

